  "title": "The Absolute Value Function",
  "body": " The Absolute Value Function   In this Lesson we solve absolute value equations and inequalities, and calculate error tolerance. Give a short lesson on using the notation to discuss distance. If you wish to cover measurement error, you could do that now.  The Activities for this Lesson are devoted to solving absolute value equations and inequalities.   Use absolute value notation to write statements about distance  Use graphs to solve absolute value equations and inequalities  Solve absolute value equations and inequalities algebraically  Express error tolerances using absolute value notation    Solving Absolute Value Equations  We'll solve the equation in three different ways: with a graph, wth a number line, and with algebra.   Solution by graphing   Graph with technology, and copy the graph onto the grid below.  grid [-10,10]x[-4,10]  Sketch the horizontal line on your graph.  Find the points on the graph of that have . (How many points are there?) What are the -coordinates of those two points? These are the solutions.   Next we'll use a number line to solve the equation.   Translate the equation into a sentence about distance.  Locate 3 on the number line below, and find two points at a distance of 5 units from 3.  number line -10 to 10  What are the -coordinates of those two points?  2(2) Point out that when using a graph, the solution appears on the -axis, and looks the same as the solution obtained on a number line.   Finally, we'll use algebra to sove the equation. Recall the definition of absolute value:   Now substitute for :  Thus, the equation has two parts:  2(3) Get students to see that we can replace by . This will be useful when we solve inequalities.  Solve each equation to get two solutions.   Solve the equation in three different ways:   By graphing .  grid [-10,10]x[-4,10]  By using a number line.  number line -10 to 10  By using algebra.     Solving Absolute Value Inequalities  We'll solve the inequality in three different ways: with a graph, wth a number line, and with algebra.   Solution by graphing   Refer to your graph of in Activity 2, and shade the points on the graph that have .  grid [-10,10]x[-4,10]  Now shade the portion of the -axis that corresponds to those points. (Are the endpoints included in the solution set?) Write your answer with interval notation.  3(1) When choosing the correct intervals on the -axis, use the same language we used when solving quadratic inequalities: are the correct -values \"between\" the boundary points, or \"outside\" of them?  Now use the graph to solve . Note that the solution set consists of two intervals.  Write your answer with interval notation.   Next, we'll use a number line.   Translate the inequality into a sentence about distance.  Locate 3 on the number line below, and shade the points closer than 5 units from 3.  number line -10 to 10  Write your answer with interval notation.  Now use the number line to solve .  number line -10 to 10  Shade the points farther than 5 units from 3. Write your answer with interval notation.   Finally, we'll solve the inequality with algebra.  This absolute value inequality is equivalent to the compound inequality . Solve the inequality and verify that you get the same solution as before.  Now we'll solve . This absolute value inequality is equivalent to the compound inequality Solve the inequality and verify that you get the same solution as before.  3(4) Most students find it difficult to follow an algebraic argument to obtain the conclusions on absolute value inequalities summarized in the box in the text. It is more effective to rely on the graphical interpretation described above to motivate the algebraic solution.    More Inequalities   Solve the inequality in two ways:   Graph on the grid and use the graph to solve the inequality. Write your answer in interval notation.  grid -10 to 10 by -4 to 10  Solve algebraically. is equivalent to   Solve the inequality in two ways   Graph on the grid and use the graph to solve the inequality. Write your answer in interval notation.  grid -10 to 10 by -4 to 10  Solve algebraically. is equivalent to or     Checkpoint   How do we write \"the distance between and 5\" in mathematical notation?  How many solutions are there to the equation , if ?  Do you prefer to solve absolute value inequalities algebraically, or with a graph? Why?   Questions for Writing or Discussion   Describe the absolute value graph.  Explain how to solve an absolute value inequality with a graph.  Find the error in this statement and correct it:  \"If , then either or .\"  What is error tolerance, and how is it used?   Concept Questions   A To find the absolute value of a number, we can   change its sign.  subtract it from zero.  find its distance from zero.  square it.   The notation means   is 3 units bigger than 5  the distance between and 5 is 3 units  5 and 3 are units apart  the distance between and 3 is 5 units   Which statement is true?   The graph of has no negative inputs.  The equation has two solutions.  Depending on , can equal or .  The graph of is a straight line.   Which statement is false?   The equation has one solution.  The statement describes an error tolerance of 0.5 units.  is equivalent to .  The solutions of form a closed interval.    "
