  "number": "5.3",
  "title": "The Natural Base",
  "body": " The Natural Base  It is difficult to explain to students before calculus why the natural base is important. This Lesson includes two interesting numerical facts about , but we have not attempted to justify its relevance to exponential functions. If you wish, you might offer the two usual pictures to the class:  area under y=1\/x tangent line to y=e^x  GOALS   Evaluate and graph the natural exponential and natural log functions  Approximate the value of  Solve exponential equations base  Model exponential growth and decay with base   Base   Recall that is an irrational number approximately equal to 2.7. Use your calculator to help you complete the table. Round values to tenths.  1(a) Students are not comfortable with , because they are not comfortable with any irrational numbers. Take every opportunity to state that is about 2.7.                                              Plot points from the table to sketch all three graphs on the grid below.  grid -2 t0 10 by -2 t0 10  On the same grid, graph , and .  1(c) Many students read \"ln x\" as \"eye-n-x.\" Point out that \"ln\" stands for natural log, so it's an ell not an eye.    Changing the Base   Because the number is universal, it is useful to express any exponential growth or decay law with base . This is easy to do.  First, notice that the laws of exponents tells that .   Follow the steps to write as   We need to replace by . That is, we need to find so that . Solve this equation for . Round your answer to tenths.  Substitute your value for into the formula for .  Check that the two formulas for give the same outputs for and .  2(b) It is very helpful to demonstrate that both forms of the function have the same graph.   Rewrite each exponential function with base :     How do you know from the formulas that the second function is decreasing?    We can also convert easily from logarithms in one base to logs in another base, using the Change of Base formula.  Suppose we want to change from base 2 to base 10. Start with and follow the steps:      Now use your rule to find . Is your answer reasonable?  How can you generalize your rule in part (b) to change from any base to a different base ?    What is ?  You have already met , an irrational number that turns up in many mathematical models, including the geometric formulas you know. It is one of the constants that describes our universe. Another such universal constant is , but its significance is not usually apparent without calculus. Here is a preview.   Use your calculator to evaluate the expression for large values of . What number does the expression approach as increases?              What number does the expression approach as increases?  Recall that . For example, . Evaluate the following expressions.        What number does the sum approach as the number of terms increases?    Checkpoint   Why would you expect the graph of to lie between the graphs of and ?  What does \"ln\" stand for in the function ?  Give decimal approximations for the constants and .  Is an increasing or a decreasing function of ? Is it concave up or down?   Questions for Writing or Discussion   Explain how to rewrite with the natural base.  Delbert says that he will begin solving the equation by computing . Is this a good strategy? Why or why not?  Explain why .  Explain why solving exponential equations in base is no harder than solving exponential equations in base 10.   Concept Questions   The value of is closest to        Which of the following is equivalent to ?        Why is the equation easier to solve than ?   8 is larger than 6.5  is a constant  There is a button for log base on the calculator, but not a button for log base 8.  Because is an irrational number.   The natural log of a fraction between 0 and 1 is   positive.  negative.  undefined.  between and .    "
