  "body": "A Quadratic Model  The idea that rectangles with the same perimeter may have different areas is new to many students. Before tackling this model, you may want to illustrate the concept by drawing several rectangles with perimeter 36 but different dimensions and areas.  A rancher has 360 yards of fence to enclose a rectangular pasture. If she uses a riverbank to border one side of the pasture, she can enclose 16,000 square yards of land. What will the dimensions of the pasture be then?  We'll solve this problem in three different ways. First, make a sketch of the pasture and the river.   Method 1: Using a Table of Values  Notice that you only have 360 yards of fence. So if you decide how wide the pasture is going to be, the length of the pasture is determined.  3a. Students find it difficult to compute the length of the rectangle when its width is known. Do several numerical examples.   Use the perimeter of the pasture, 360 yards, to write a formula for the length of the pasture in terms of its width. (Be careful: remember that one side of the pasture does not need any fence!)  Length =  Make a table that shows the areas of pastures of various widths.    Width  Length  Area  10  340  3400                     Continue the table until you find the pasture whose area is 16,000 square yards.  Dimensions of pasture:   Method 2: Using a Graph  On your sketch of the pasture in part (1), label the width of the pasture as .   Write an expression for the length of the pasture if its width is . (Hint: How did you compute the length of the pasture in Method 1?  Length =  Write an expression for the area of the pasture if its width is .  Area =  Use technology to graph the equation for . If you use a calculator, set the window at Then copy the graph onto the grid.  Grid [0,200]x[0,20]  3(2)c. Emphasize the fact that the graph shows how the area changes as we change the width of the rectangle; the length of the rectangle does not appear in this graph.  On the graph, find the pasture of area 16,000 square yards. Label the correct point on your graph.  Dimensions of pasture:   Method 3: Using an Equation   Refer to steps (a) and (b) of Method 2, and write an equation for the area of the pasture in terms of its width .  Area =  3(3)a. Once again, students have trouble writing algebraic expressions for the length and the area of the pasture.  Substitute and solve your equation algebraically.  Dimensions of pasture:  Which of the three methods do you prefer? Why?    "
