  "title": "Quadratic Inequalities",
  "body": " Quadratic Inequalities   In this lesson we solve quadratic inequalities graphically and algebraically, and write their solutions with interval notation. Note that our algebraic technique uses properties of the parabola; we do not use a \"sign graph\" to solve inequalities.  GOALS   Write compound inequalities  Use interval notation  Solve quadratic inequalities graphically  Solve quadratic inequalities algebraically   Equal, Less Than, Greater Than  Here are three copies of the graph of .  You might want to preface this Activity by having students use trial and error to find solutions to , and plot their solutions on a number line on the board. (Remind them to try both positive and negative values, and fractions.) Then do the same for .    On the first graph, we'll solve the equation . (Remember that .)   Find two points on the graph with . Put dots there.  What are the -coordinates of those points?  The solutions are:   On the second graph, we'll solve the inequality .  Explain that because the graph shows points for which , the solutions to are just the -coordinates of points on the graph with . You will need to go over this reasoning on every problem.   Mark all points on the graph that have .  On the -axis, mark the -coordinates of all those points.  Write an inequality to describe the portion of the -axis marked.   On the third graph, we'll solve the inequality .   Mark all points on the graph that have .  On the -axis, mark the -coordinates of all those points.  Write two inequalities to describe the portion of the -axis marked.  Emphasize that because the solution set consists of two intervals, we need two inequalities to describe it. Also explain why is not correct.     Solving Quadratic Inequalities Graphically  Here are two copies of the graph of .    Use the first graph to solve .  (Hint: Notice that one of the -intercepts is . Think of the factored form of the equation. What is the other factor?)  2(2) Start using the language \"between the -intercepts or outside the -intercepts\" or something similar to describe the choice of which part of the -axis provides the solutions.  Use the second graph to solve .   Mark all points on the graph that have .  On the -axis, mark the -coordinates of all those points.  Write two inequalities to describe the portion of the -axis marked.   Solve the inequality .   Rewrite the inequality so that the right side is zero.  2(3) This problem also provides an opportunity to review graphing a parabola by hand. Notice that the coefficient of is negative, so expect some confusion about finding the -intercepts versus finding the -coordinate of the vertex.  Graph the equation .  -intercept:  -intercepts:  (Solve   grid [-4,10]x[-6,26]  Use the graph to solve the inequality. Write the solution with interval notation.  2(3) Take a few minutes to give examples of interval notation. In this Lesson, we use to indicate the union of two intervals, but we do not discuss intersection of sets.     Solving Inequalities Algebraically  To solve a quadratic inequality, we must answer two questions:  What are the -intercepts of the related graph?  Does the parabola open up or down?  Remember that you may need to use extraction of roots or the quadratic formula to find the -intercepts of a parabola.     graph of y=x^2-4x-5 on [-3,7]x[-10,10]  Use the graph to solve Show the solution on the graph. Write your answer in interval notation.  3(2) Remind students that once we have found the -intercepts as boundary points for our solution set, we only need to decide whether the solutions lie between the intercepts or outside them -- we don't need any other information about the parabola (such as the vertex). So we only need to know if the parabola opens up or down.   Follow the steps to solve .    Find the -intercepts of .  Does the graph of open up or down? Make a rough sketch of the graph, and label the -intercepts.  Use the graph to solve the inequality. Write your answer in interval notation.   grid [-5,5]x[-10,10]  Solve the inequality .    Write the inequality in standard form.  Find the -intercepts of the corresponding graph.  Make a rough sketch of the graph. Use the graph to solve the inequality. Write your answer with interval notation.   grid [-6,6]x[-20,4]  Problems (3) and (4) point out that sometimes we have to use extraction of roots or the quadratic formula to find the -intercepts.  Solve the inequality .    Write the inequality in standard form.  Find the -intercepts of the corresponding graph. (Use the quadratic formula.)  Make a rough sketch of the graph. Use the graph to solve the inequality. Write your answer with interval notation.   10x10 grid    Checkpoint   Activity 1, we saw that for any value of , the value of is either , , or .  Suppose you have a point with -coordinate 8 on the graph of an equation. How do you find the -value that produces in the equation?  When you solve a quadratic inequality with a graph, how do you find the boundary points of the solution interval(s)?  Use a graph to explain why the inequality has no solution.   Questions for Writing or Discussion   Explain how to use a graph to find the -value that produces a particular -value for a quadratic equation.  Explain why you cannot write the solutions to as a single inequality.  Explain how to use a graph to solve .  Describe how to use interval notation.   Concept Questions   What are the solutions of the inequality ?     All real numbers  No solutions   Which points on the parabola do we need to know to solve the inequality ?   The -intercepts  The -intercept  The vertex  All of these   Why do we need to know whether the parabola in question 2 opens up or down?   To decide whether to use or in the solution.  To decide whether the solutions lie between the -intercepts or outside them.  To decide whether the solutions are positive or negative.  To help us find the -intercepts.   What does the notation mean?   The point with -coordinate and -coordinate .  or  All real numbers between and , excluding the endpoints.  All real numbers between and , including the endpoints.    "
