  "id": "sec-7-2",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "sec-7-2.html",
  "type": "Section",
  "number": "7.2",
  "title": "Graphs of Polynomial Functions",
  "body": " Graphs of Polynomial Functions   Graphing polynomials by hand is not really a useful skill (especially without calculus to find relative extrema), because graphing software is readily available. This Lesson presents a more scaffolded version of Homework Problems 69 and 70, which introduce the idea of using polynomials to approximate transcendental functions. Students can use a graphing calcultor or an on-line graphing tool such as Desmos to create their graphs.  GOALS   Approximate a function by a polynomial  Recognize the long-term behavior of a polynomial of degree  Apply the Factor Theorem to locate the zeros of a polynomial   Approximating  Polynomials are easy to evaluate (especially with a computer) because they involve only the four arithmetic operations. And because they can take on such a variety of shapes, they are often used to approximate other functions.   Graph the functions on the interval . For what values of does it appear that would be a good approximation for ?  Ask the class what they consider a \"good approximation.\"  Use your calculator to fill in the table of values.                                     Check that students understand why gives the error in the approximation.  The difference is the error in the approximation.    Polynomial Approximation   We can reduce the error in our approximation by using a polynomial of higher degree. Replace on your graph by What is the largest error for on the interval ?  The th degree polynomial for approximating is where Graph and on the interval . (You will need to include larger -values.) What is the error in approximating by ?  Graph on the interval , with -values from to . Use the graph to estimate the largest interval on which the error is less than 0.01.  What is the smallest value of for which is less than 0.01 on the interval  What conclusions can you draw from your investigation? Write a short paragraph summarizing your results.    Shapes of Polynomials  For each description, write the equation of the polynomial of smallest degree that has the given features. Use your graphing tool to check your work.  Ask the class how they can find (without graphing) the correct lead coefficient to give the desired -intercept.    -intercepts at and ; -intercept at  zeros at and , double zero at ; -intercept at  double zeros at and ; -intercept at  zero at , double zero at , triple zero at ; -intercept at    Checkpoint   Can you factor the polynomial in Activity 1? How do you know?  Can you factor the polynomial in Activity 2? How do you know?  How can you alter the equation for a polynomial to change its -intercept from to ?  What does the multiplicity of a zero tell you about the shape of the graph?   Questions for Writing or Discussion   Explain how you might estimate if you have only a four-function calculator.  Why does the graph of a polynomial have the shape it does near a double zero?  How may the graph of a polynomial differ from the graph of a power function of the same degree?  Describe the difference between the graphs of and .   Concept Questions   At an inflection point, a graph changes   direction  concavity  sign  all of these   The graph of which type of polynomial must have an -intercept?   quadratic  cubic  quartic  none of these   Without graphing, how can you tell that the polynomial has no -intercepts?   It is quartic.  All of its coefficients are positive.  It cannot be factored further.  It has no turning points.   The long-term behavior of a polynomial of degree   depends on the number of its zeros.  is the same as the behavior of .  may have a horizontal asymptote.  cannot be determined without a graph.     "
