  "id": "sec-7-3",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "sec-7-3.html",
  "type": "Section",
  "number": "7.3",
  "title": "Complex Numbers",
  "body": " Complex Numbers   In this Lesson students practice graphing and performing operations on complex numbers while exploring complex roots. Begin by describing the complex plane and show students how to plot points for complex numbers. For these Activities, we will use \"arrows\" to locate points, so that students can more clearly see angles and rotations.  GOALS   Perform operations on complex numbers  Plot complex numbers in the complex plane    Products  The figure shows the graph of as the tip of an arrow starting at the origin.  arrow on grid from (0,0) t0 (3,2)    What happens to the arrow when we multiply by a real number? Compute and draw an arrow on the same grid to represent it.  Multiplying a complex number by a real number changes the of the arrow.  What if we multiply a complex number by ? Compute and draw an arrow on the grid to represent it.  Multiplying a complex number by rotates the arrow by °.  Discuss with students how they can know that the rotation is 90°.    More Products   Multiply your value for from Activity 1 by another factor of , and draw an arrow to represent it.   What do you notice about the result?  Why does this happen?  Multiply your result from part (a) by one more factor of , and draw an arrow to represent it.  What do you notice about the four arrows?    Roots   Find two square roots of in the complex numbers.  Find two square roots of in the complex numbers.  Draw arrows for the four complex numbers  2 by 2 grid, axes x and iy  Raise each complex number in part (c) to the fourth power.  How many fourth roots of have you found in the complex numbers? Do you think there could be others?  This Activity introduces the idea that the complex roots of a number are equally spaced around the origin. You might need to prompt them to notice this.    More Roots   Draw an arrow on the grid to represent (Use your calculator to evaluate .)  1 by 1 grid, scaled in tenths  Compute  Students may have difficulty with the computations. Circulate and offer help, and if necessary demonstrate for the class.  Compute  Draw another arrow on the grid to represent  Compute  Compute  How many fourth roots of have you found in the complex numbers?    Yet More Roots   Draw an arrow on the grid to represent  1 by 1 grid, scaled in tenths  Compute  Compute  Let from part (b). Compute What do you notice?  Compute Compare to your calculation in part (b)>  How many cube roots of have you found in the complex numbers? List them.  You might offer a wrap-up stating the conclusions you hope students will draw from the Lesson.    Checkpoint   From your work here, state a conjecture about the number of complex th roots a real number has.  How are those roots located in the complex plane?  What is an easy way to compute ?   Questions for Writing or Discussion   Explain how to divide one complex number by another.  What does the Fundamental Theorem of Algebra tell us? Give an example.  Describe how multiplying complex numbers is like multiplying binomials.  What can you say about the graph of a quadratic function whose zeros are complex numbers?   Concept Questions   How many fourth roots does have in the complex numbers?   2  4  1  none   What is the imaginary unit?        What is the product of with its complex conjugate?        If is a solution to a polynomial equation, then so is:         "
